<p>The second query above would traditionally require PostgreSQL to scan through all index entries or fall back to a sequential scan because it doesn&#x27;t specify the leading <code>region</code> column.</p>
<h3 id="the-skip-scan-solution" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>The Skip Scan Solution</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#the-skip-scan-solution" aria-label="Link to The Skip Scan Solution"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>Skip scan solves this by allowing PostgreSQL to efficiently navigate the index structure even when prefix columns are missing from the query. It does this by identifying distinct values in the unspecified prefix columns and performing targeted scans for each value.</p>
