hildren:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n68:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Now let's populate the table with sample data that demonstrates the skip scan benefits:\"}]\n6a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The above SQL statement generates 100,000 rows of sample sales data with random regions, categories, product names, sale dates throughout 2025, and amounts. This dataset will allow us to test the skip scan functionality effectively.\"}]\n6b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"After inserting the data, we can update the statistics for the query planner to ensure it has the latest information about the table and index:\"}]\n6d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"This creates a dataset with three regions, four categories, and dates spread throughout 2025, providing a good test case for skip scan functionality.\"}]\n6e:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"demonstrating-skip-scan-in-action\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Demonstrating Skip Scan in Action\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\",\"href\":\"#demonstrating-skip-scan-in-action\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Demonstrating Skip Scan in Action\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Let's see how PostgreSQL 18's skip scan feature improves query performance for different scenarios.\"}]\n70:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"basic-skip-scan-example\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Basic Skip Scan Example\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#basic-skip-scan-example\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Basic Skip Scan Example\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n71:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"First, let's examine a query that benefits from skip scan:\"}]\n73:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"In PostgreSQL 18, this query can use the skip scan optimization to efficiently use the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"idx_sales_analytics\"}],\" index even though it doesn't specify the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"region\"}],\" column.\"]}]\n74:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The query planner will identify the distinct region values and perform targeted scans for each region where the category and date conditions match.\"}]\n75:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"comparing-with-traditional-approach\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Comparing with Traditional Approach\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#comparing-with-traditional-approach\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Comparing with Traditional Approach\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n76:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"To see the difference, you can compare this with a query that includes all prefix columns:\"}]\n78:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Both queries should now show similar performance characteristics, whereas in previous PostgreSQL versions, the first query would be significantly slower.\"}]\n79:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"when-skip-scan-provides-the-most-benefit\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"When Skip Scan Provides the Most Benefit\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#when-skip-scan-provides-the-most-benefit\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to When Skip Scan Provides the Most Benefit\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Skip scan optimization is most effective in specific scenarios. Understanding these helps you design better indexes and queries.\"}]\n7b:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"low-cardinali"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"ty-leading-columns\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Low Cardinality Leading Columns\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#low-cardinality-leading-columns\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Low Cardinality Leading Columns\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Skip scan works best when the leading columns that you're omitting have relatively few distinct values:\"}]\n7e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The optimization works by essentially performing separate index scans for each distinct value in the omitted leading columns, so fewer distinct values mean fewer separate scans.\"}]\n7f:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"monitoring-skip-scan-usage\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Monitoring Skip Scan Usage\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#monitoring-skip-scan-usage\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Monitoring Skip Scan Usage\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n80:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"PostgreSQL 18 provides \",[\"$\",\"$L52\",null,{\"href\":\"/postgresql/postgresql-18/enhanced-explain\",\"children\":\"enhanced EXPLAIN\"}],\" output that helps you understand when skip scan is being used.\"]}]\n81:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"enhanced-explain-output\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Enhanced EXPLAIN Output\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#enhanced-explain-output\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Enhanced EXPLAIN Output\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n82:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"When examining query plans, look for indicators that skip scan is being used:\"}]\n84:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The output will show how PostgreSQL is handling the index scan and whether it's using skip scan optimization. You'll see information about the number of index lookups and how efficiently the query is using the available index.\"}]\n85:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"performance-considerations\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Performance Considerations\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#performance-considerations\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Performance Considerations\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n86:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"While skip scan significantly improves query performance in many scenarios, it's important to understand its characteristics and limitations.\"}]\n87:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"when-skip-scan-may-not-help\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"When Skip Scan May Not Help\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#when-skip-scan-may-not-help\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to When Skip Scan May Not Help\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n88:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Skip scan isn't beneficial in all situations. Let's examine the scenarios where PostgreSQL's query planner will choose other strategies.\"}]\n89:[\"$\",\"h4\",null,{\"id\":\"high-cardinality-leading-columns\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"High Cardinality Leading Columns\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#high-cardinality-leading-columns\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to High Cardinality Leading Columns\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52\",\"children\":\"$4a:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n8a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"High cardinality means the leading column in your index has many distinct values.\"}]\n8b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"When the leading column has too many distinct values, skip scan becomes inefficient:\"}]\n8d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"With an index on \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"(user_id, event_type, event_date)\"}],\", skip scan would need to perform separate scans for each \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"user_id\"}],\" value - potentially millions of them. This makes it slower than alternatives. In such cases, PostgreSQL will likely choose a sequential scan or bitmap index scan instead.\"]}]\n8e:[\"$\",\"h4\",null,{\"id\":\"large-result-sets\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Large Result Sets\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#large-result-sets\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Large Result Sets\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 